<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ambrose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 339–397</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ambrose, Ambrose of Milan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ambrose was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 374 and one of the four original Doctors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a>. A Roman governor chosen bishop by popular acclaim, he opposed the Arians, defended the Church's independence from emperors, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> do penance after the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 390, and baptised Augustine. He became the patron saint of Milan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambrose.jpg" alt="Ambrose" width="306" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmbroseOfMilan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the Church Fathers, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, John Chrysostom, <b>Ambrose</b>, Jerome and Augustine of Hippo in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>When Theodosius I dismantled the old cults, the senator Quintus Aurelius Symmachus protested in a letter, and <b>Ambrose</b>, Bishop of Milan, urged the rejection of his plea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian writers such as Tertullian, <b>Ambrose</b> and Augustine of Hippo, whose Confessions and City of God shaped Western thought, made Latin the language of theology.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish priest Bernard Callan put it into Irish around 1800, John Newton made a loose Protestant version, and <b>Ambrose</b> Bierce a satirical one in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Gregory of Nyssa wrote fifteen Homilies on the Song, comparing the bride to the soul and the groom to God, and Augustine, following <b>Ambrose of Milan</b>, read it as the wedding of Christ and the Church.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition ascribes it to <b>Saint Ambrose</b> (died 397) or Saint Augustine (died 430), and it is sometimes called the Ambrosian Hymn, though Ambrose's authorship is unlikely.</blockquote><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum</a></li></ul></section>
